-- title:
--   ℚ is algebraically closed in the modular function field
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ N$ denote the intermediate field of the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ the set `divisorExpansions N`, namely those Laurent series of the form `qExpand ℚ d jq` for nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$ — that is, the $q$-expansions of $j$ substituted at $q^{d}$ for $d \mid N$. The theorem asserts: for every element $x$ of this intermediate field which is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ (in the sense that some nonzero polynomial over $\mathbb{Q}$ annihilates $x$ in the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ N$), there exists a rational number $c$ with $x$ equal to the image of $c$ under the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ N$. In other words, $\mathbb{Q}$ is algebraically closed inside the field generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$; the algebraic elements of this modular function field are exactly the rational constants.
--
--   This is the statement that the field of constants of the modular curve $X_0(N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is $\mathbb{Q}$ itself, phrased for the concrete $q$-expansion model of the function field. It is used in the integral-closedness arguments for the chart algebras of the Igusa scheme, where rational constants must be separated from genuine modular functions after base change.
